id: pm-9smp7j
title: pm extension --init and pm package init are divergent scaffold generators behind the same grammar; the extension path emits no tests and an unpublishable manifest
description: "Two scaffold generators are reachable through near-identical grammar and produce materially different quality. Verified against dist build 2026.7.25 by running both with identical arguments.\n\npm extension --init <path> --capability commands emits 5 files: index.ts, manifest.json, package.json, README.md, tsconfig.json. Its package.json is the single line {\"type\": \"module\"} with no name, version, license, scripts, engines, peerDependencies or pm block. There is no test file and no .gitignore.\n\npm package --init <path> --capability commands emits 7 files: the same five plus .gitignore and index.test.ts, and its package.json is a complete publishable manifest with name, version, keywords, engines, typecheck/test scripts, peerDependencies on @unbrained/pm-cli, devDependencies, and the full pm block (aliases, extensions, docs, examples, catalog with display_name/category/summary/tags). The generated manifests are byte-identical apart from the name, and index.ts differs.\n\nConsequence for the stated goal of making package creation as easy as possible: an agent or author who reasons 'I am building an extension' reaches for the extension noun, which is also where --capability is documented in pm extension --help, and lands on the degraded generator. Its next_steps never mention that pm package init exists or that it produces the fuller starter, so nothing routes the author to the better path. docs/EXTENSIONS.md describes the rich behaviour (per-capability starters with a colocated node:test suite built on the SDK assertRegistered*/runRegistered*ForTest helpers) under the package noun, so the extension path silently diverges from its own documentation. The result is a starter with zero tests in a project that enforces 100% coverage internally and publishes a dedicated @unbrained/pm-cli/sdk/testing subpath, and a package.json that cannot be published or installed as a pm package without being hand-rewritten to the shape every real unbraind pm-* repository uses.\n\nRelated evidence for pm-quzx: the good generator itself mints an unbounded peer range, peerDependencies {\"@unbrained/pm-cli\": \">=2026.6.24\"}, so the scaffold is an upstream source of the unbounded-range pattern that item already tracks."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags[3]: "area:extensions",dx,scaffold
created_at: "2026-07-25T21:13:51.838Z"
updated_at: "2026-07-25T23:43:47.292Z"
closed_at: "2026-07-25T23:36:15.515Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 180
acceptance_criteria: "The extension and package scaffold paths share one generator so identical arguments produce identical output, or the extension path is explicitly narrowed to a documented sub-case and its output and next_steps state which fuller path to use instead. Every scaffold path that produces an installable artifact emits a colocated test built on the public SDK testing helpers and a package.json that pm install accepts without hand editing. Documentation in docs/EXTENSIONS.md matches the behaviour of both nouns. A regression test asserts scaffold parity between the two entry points for every --capability value."
parent: pm-ugqx
risk: low
confidence: high
resolution: Unified package and extension scaffold generation behind one publishable TypeScript artifact builder for all ten capabilities.
expected_result: "Both init spellings emit installable, testable, publishable packages with identical metadata and runtime behavior."
actual_result: "Both spellings emit the same seven-file artifact, generated tests/typechecks pass, and independently packed consumer installs and dispatches both forms."
